Source offer
------------

The container image is built entirely from upstream source. The build clones
https://github.com/Cawlumm/lyftr and checks out the ref given by the
LYFTR_REF build argument, then compiles the Go backend from backend/ and the
web assets from web/ at that ref. Nothing is pulled from a prebuilt upstream
image.

The Dockerfile pins LYFTR_REF to a release tag rather than a moving branch, so
the artifact is identifiable:

  Tag:    v0.1.0-beta.6
  Commit: 5dae7c50b2dda877f394a247272ccfc3b9d9d32d
  URL:    https://github.com/Cawlumm/lyftr/tree/v0.1.0-beta.6

Because the image builds the backend and the web assets from source at that
ref, the Corresponding Source for the Lyftr code in the image is that upstream
tag (equivalently, that commit). Anyone who receives the image can obtain the
exact sources from the URL above, or by running this repository's build with
LYFTR_REF set to that tag.


Third-party components (build and runtime images)
-------------------------------------------------

The image is assembled from the following base images. Each carries the
license terms of its own upstream project and of the distribution packages it
contains; this list names the primary projects involved and is not a complete
package manifest.

  alpine/git (source-fetch stage)
    Image:   https://hub.docker.com/r/alpine/git
    Git:     https://git-scm.com, GPL-2.0
    Alpine:  https://alpinelinux.org

  golang:1.26-alpine (backend build stage)
    Image:   https://hub.docker.com/_/golang
    Go:      https://go.dev, BSD-3-Clause
    Alpine:  https://alpinelinux.org

  node:20-alpine (web asset build stage)
    Image:   https://hub.docker.com/_/node
    Node.js: https://nodejs.org, MIT
    npm:     https://npmjs.com, Artistic-2.0
    Alpine:  https://alpinelinux.org

  alpine:3.19 (runtime stage)
    Image:   https://hub.docker.com/_/alpine
    Alpine Linux: https://alpinelinux.org
    musl libc:    https://musl.libc.org, MIT
    BusyBox:      https://busybox.net, GPL-2.0

The runtime stage additionally installs these Alpine packages:

  python3          https://python.org, Python Software Foundation License
  ca-certificates  Mozilla CA certificate bundle, MPL-2.0
  wget             https://gnu.org/software/wget, GPL-3.0-or-later

Go and JavaScript dependency trees are not enumerated here. They are resolved
during the build from upstream's own manifests and lock files at the pinned
ref named above:

  backend/go.mod, backend/go.sum   the Go modules linked into the backend
                                   binary
  web/package.json                 the web app's declared dependencies
  package-lock.json (repo root)    the npm workspaces lock file that resolves
                                   them, covering web/ and the shared packages

Those files are the authoritative record of the third-party libraries linked
into the backend binary and bundled into the web assets, and each such library
remains under its own license. Upstream moved to an npm workspaces layout with
a single root lock file, so the root lock file is the record that applies at
the recommended pin, whatever path a given build copies its npm inputs from.

The packaging code in this repository (auth_proxy.py, start.sh) uses only the
Python standard library and BusyBox shell utilities; it bundles no third-party
code.


Third-party data (fetched at runtime, not redistributed here)
-------------------------------------------------------------

Neither dataset below is vendored into this repository or baked into the
image. Both are retrieved over the network by the running app, so both require
outbound network access. Without it the exercise library stays empty and food
search does not work.

  free-exercise-db
    Source:  https://github.com/yuhonas/free-exercise-db
    License: The Unlicense (public domain)
    Use:     the seeded exercise library. The exercise metadata (names,
             muscles, equipment, categories, instructions) is downloaded from
             this dataset the first time the app starts with an empty
             database and is then stored in the app's database. The
             illustration images are not copied: each exercise record holds a
             URL into the same dataset, which the browser loads at display
             time.

  Open Food Facts
    Source:  https://world.openfoodfacts.org
    License: Open Database License (ODbL) for the database, with product
             data contributed by the Open Food Facts community
    Use:     food name search and barcode lookup are proxied to Open Food
             Facts at request time. Nutrition values a user chooses to log
             are stored locally in the app's database; users who redistribute
             data derived from Open Food Facts must comply with the ODbL,
             including its attribution and share-alike terms.


